  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A backdoor cold front is expected to bring showers \nand a possible thunderstorm across the Finger Lakes and Eastern Lake \nOntario region through this evening.\n\nA long wave trough will remain across the Northeast and northern \nAtlantic Ocean through Monday. An embedded shortwave trough over \nnorthern Quebec will move south across the eastern Great Lakes \nregion through tonight. Increasing moisture and an approaching cold \nfront will support the development of showers for northern New York \ninto the Finger Lakes region. Weak instability is expected to \ndevelop inland from Lake Ontario today and a thunderstorm or two \ncan't be ruled out. Spotty rainfall amounts of less than a \ntenth of an inch are expected.\n\nLow-level dry air and steepening low-level lapse rates ahead of the \ncold front will support wind gusts up to 30 mph in any heavy\nshowers. Ahead of the front, wind gusts up to 25 mph will \ncontinue from the Finger Lakes region to Oswego county this \nafternoon. Patchy fog is possible across the Black River Valley \novernight.\n\nElsewhere across the forecast area, mid-high level clouds will \nincrease through the evening. Clouds will then diminish in coverage \novernight. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Unsettled weather is expected to return next\nweekend.\n\nThe upper level pattern suggests that the ridge will break down\nnext weekend. Multiple deterministic models indicate that \nshortwave troughs will move near the forecast area. Deep \nsoutherly flow from the Gulf may result in increased moisture \nacross the region, subsequently increasing the chances for \nshowers and thunderstorms through the weekend. The highest \nprobability for any activity will be inland from the lakes \nduring peak heating.",
